1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,810
Old Faithful's eruptions.

2
00:00:01,930 --> 00:00:04,790
Data first, the closest bell is this one.

3
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:06,110
Swap the order.

4
00:00:06,510 --> 00:00:10,820
If closeness is a distance, order can't matter: it stays at one.

5
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:15,093
Or does the closest bell move to two, or to three?

6
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:21,123
The D is KL divergence, named for Kullback and Leibler.

7
00:00:21,323 --> 00:00:25,043
Every maximum-likelihood fit shrinks it with the data first.

8
00:00:25,243 --> 00:00:29,243
Much of Bayesian machine learning shrinks it with the order flipped.

9
00:00:29,443 --> 00:00:31,233
It's often called a distance.

10
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:33,060
So treat it as one.

11
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,980
Data first, this bell is point seven six bits away.

12
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:42,700
Shift it or stretch it, and that number only grows, so bell A is the closest.

13
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,290
But a distance reads the same both ways.

14
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:53,050
Put the bell first, and the same two curves are one point seven bits apart, more than twice as far.

15
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:58,000
Only the order changed, so the formula must care about order.

16
00:00:58,466 --> 00:01:01,446
To see why, take one eruption two minutes long.

17
00:01:01,746 --> 00:01:04,956
The data's curve stands this high there: call it p.

18
00:01:05,406 --> 00:01:06,796
The bell gives it q,

19
00:01:07,096 --> 00:01:08,606
about a quarter as much.

20
00:01:08,906 --> 00:01:11,156
Double q twice and you reach p.

21
00:01:11,606 --> 00:01:16,966
So log base two of p over q is about two: two doublings, or two bits.

22
00:01:18,666 --> 00:01:20,286
Do that at every length.

23
00:01:21,186 --> 00:01:26,436
Then weight each by p, how often eruptions last that long, and add it all up.

24
00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:28,906
Where the bell rises above the data,

25
00:01:29,356 --> 00:01:31,086
the grey dips pay a little back.

26
00:01:31,586 --> 00:01:34,846
That's the divergence: the same point seven six.

27
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:38,610
Try a bell that skips the short eruptions.

28
00:01:38,910 --> 00:01:43,230
At two minutes, it falls short by eighteen and a half doublings.

29
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:50,340
More than a third of all eruptions are short, so the bill comes to five point eight bits.

30
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:52,970
Now keep that bill.

31
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:55,330
For a second bill, swap the order:

32
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:57,640
every p trades places with q.

33
00:01:57,940 --> 00:02:01,200
The bell supplies the lengths, and the data judges them.

34
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:03,070
Which bell wins now?

35
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:06,463
Take bell B first.

36
00:02:06,613 --> 00:02:11,563
It almost never imagines a short eruption, so ignoring them costs nothing.

37
00:02:11,683 --> 00:02:13,293
It pays point six two.

38
00:02:14,193 --> 00:02:19,783
Bell A imagines eruptions in the gap and the tails, where the data has almost none.

39
00:02:19,983 --> 00:02:21,633
That's its one point seven.

40
00:02:22,966 --> 00:02:26,326
Now let bell A slide downhill on the bell-first bill.

41
00:02:26,446 --> 00:02:32,456
As that bill drains, the other swells, and the bell settles into B, less than half as wide.

42
00:02:32,756 --> 00:02:35,996
Flip the order, and the closest bell moves to three.

43
00:02:37,633 --> 00:02:43,513
Maximum likelihood puts the data first, so a one-bell fit stretches over every hump.

44
00:02:44,013 --> 00:02:51,883
Variational inference approximates a Bayesian posterior with the model first, so it tends to settle on one hump.

45
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:59,293
Blei and colleagues note that variational inference generally underestimates posterior variance.

46
00:02:59,693 --> 00:03:08,623
In a real ad-conversion model, its average for one effect matched MCMC, but its spread was only a fifth as wide.

47
00:03:09,633 --> 00:03:15,603
Against raw points, with the bell first, every score is infinite, so the data must be smoothed.

48
00:03:15,903 --> 00:03:21,253
Smooth it more heavily and the gap partly fills: the best fit spreads almost back to A.

49
00:03:23,066 --> 00:03:26,146
Bell A never misses an eruption that happens.

50
00:03:26,646 --> 00:03:29,786
Bell B almost never imagines one that doesn't.

